From 87ffeaad6a58181c4258d38ef2f901f8be2a3e87 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cynthia Condra <cynthiacondra@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 15:30:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Evaluate the GLMM log-likelihood at the dispersion the fit
 actually uses

For families with an estimated dispersion parameter (Gamma, inverse Gaussian,
gaussian with a non-identity link), glmResp::Laplace() added the family's aic()
value, which evaluates the log-density at a plain deviance/n plug-in dispersion.
The fit itself (PIRLS working weights, profilePhi()) and sigma() use the
profiled phi, which with the default glmerControl(disp_dof_correction = TRUE)
is deviance/(n - rank[X, Z]). logLik(), AIC(), BIC() and anova() therefore
reported a value that is not the Laplace approximation at the fitted
parameters: for a Gamma(log) random-intercept model with n = 320 and
rank[X, Z] = 42 the reported log-likelihood was 2.3 units above both the exact
marginal log-likelihood and an independent Laplace approximation evaluated at
the fitted parameters, while disp_dof_correction = FALSE agreed to 0.01.

Hand aic() the deviance value that makes its internal plug-in equal to phi.
The existing consistency test in test-gamma_glmm_bias.R recomputed -2 logLik
at deviance/n; it now uses sigma(fit)^2 and covers both settings of
disp_dof_correction. NEWS entry added.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01TaHntBDKuZJpMAAMenkC44
